    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Round tables can be imagined as points referred to their centers, and the question of how many ways to sit 9 people around a round table can be understood as finding the number of different ways to choose any 9/2 points around a circle, i.e., choose any n points from a circle of r (here $n = 9/2$, $r = 9$), the answer to this problem is $\\boxed{\\frac{9!}{2 \\cdot 9} = 36}$. The reasoning is as follows: Since people sitting around the table can be rotated unchangingly, as long as the relative order of people is unchanged, they can be regarded as identical.\nWhen 9 people sit around a round table, there are a total of 8! ways to arrange the 8 seats occupied by people. Since rotations of the same arrangement can occur and it is allowed;\n\ntwo people sitting together can be treated as a block\nthus: 8! people can sit around a round table in $\\boxed{36}$ ways.",
