    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The question is asking us to determine the number of ways to arrange 9 people around a table. To do this, we can use a concept called symmetric groupology. \n\nThe symmetric group is a group, denoted S(9), which consists of all possible ways to permute the elements of a set of size 9. In this case, let n = 9. Since there are n elements, there are n! possible permutations. In this case, the factorial of 9 is 9! = 9 * 8 * 7 * 6 * 5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 362,880. \n\nHowever, since the problem states that two seatings are considered the same if one is a rotation of the other, we need to divide the total number of permutations by n, which is 9 in this case. This is because each seating arrangement can be rotated n times to look exactly like another seating arrangement. \n\nTherefore, the number of unique seatings is 362,880 / 9 = 40,320. \n\nSo, there are 40,320 ways for 9 people to sit around a round table.",
